#
# prima-materia — Japanese Zen
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-buddhist.ttl with no node. What was
# opened is the second of the three works the registration names, Hakuin's
# Yasenkanna, read complete in the Japanese, the preface of 1757 and the main
# text, and in two English translations by two translators. The two other works
# of the registration stay unopened and are declared below, together with two
# further works of Hakuin's on which the Yasenkanna story also stands.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practice that makes the illness and the illness; the two contemplations that
# are said to cure it and what they are said to bring about; the practices the
# hermit quotes from Chinese authors; what must come first; the cautions; what
# the text says a failure looks like and the term it sets; the signs; and the
# powers named only to be refused.
#
# Citation. The Japanese is cited by the printed page of Shaku Dokutan's edition
# in the collection Hakuin zenji hōgoroku. The transcription read is that of
# Japanese Wikisource, made from the National Diet Library scan of that
# edition; the decisive passages were checked against the scan itself (see the
# note at the work). Waddell's translation is cited by the page of The Eastern
# Buddhist, Leggett's by the page of the parallel compilation in which it was
# read, because his book was not reachable.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:Yasenkanna a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Yasenkanna, Idle talk on a night boat, of Hakuin Ekaku"@en, "Yasenkanna, Müßiges Gespräch auf einem Nachtboot, von Hakuin Ekaku"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-16 in the Japanese: the preface signed by Hunger and Cold, the Master of Poverty Hermitage, dated the twenty-fifth day of the first month of Hōreki 7 (1757), and the main text to its closing line, in the edition of Shaku Dokutan, Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 12-33. The title page, seen in the scan, names the editor as 釋毒譚 and the publisher; it carries no year, and the year 1916 is the catalogue's and the transcriber's and is not taken over as read. The text was read in the transcription of Japanese Wikisource, pages 11 to 21 of the file NDL925789 part 1, which marks every page as not yet proofread; the passages on which the term, the share of the cured and the butter method stand (pp. 16-17 and 28-29) were compared with the scan of the same pages and agree."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators. Norman Waddell, Hakuin's Yasenkanna, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 79-119, read complete with its introduction and notes; the journal's publisher, Ōtani University, serves the file in its own repository, record 8339. Waddell translates the text of Yoshizawa Katsuhiro's edition (Kyoto: Zenbunka Kenkyūsho 2000) and gives the variants of the two other versions in his notes. Trevor Leggett's translation, which the compiler places in A Second Zen Reader, was read in Frederic Lecut's parallel compilation Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (2011), which prints Leggett's text in a column of its own; it covers the main text and not the preface, so every node standing on the preface stands on the Japanese and on Waddell alone. Leggett's book itself lies at archive.org only under loan restriction (tigerscavetransl0000trev, tigerscavetransl0000unse), and its title page was not seen. The Shaw and Schiffer translation of the preface, Monumenta Nipponica 13 (1957), is not free and was not read."@en ;
    skos:note "The full-text layer of the 1934-35 complete works, Hakuin oshō zenshū, at archive.org (hakuinoshozenshu00hakuuoft to 08) is unusable for this work: the vertical print was read across the columns and none of the key terms of the text, 夜船, 軟酥, 内觀, 禪病, occurs in any of the eight volumes. That is a property of those uploads; the edition itself was not needed."@en ;
    skos:note "The frame of the work is disputed and the dispute is not about the method. Waddell's introduction (pp. 82-85) reports that the hermit Hakuyū existed, a recluse of the Shirakawa hills named Ishikawa Jishun, and that a temple death register found by Itō Kazuo in 1960 records his death from a fall in the seventh month of 1709, half a year before the visit the work dates to the first month of 1710; that the title alludes to a saying used of a man who pretends to have seen a place he has not; and that students of the question generally hold that the visit did not take place, while Hakuin's own illness and his cure by these methods are not in doubt. Hakuin's student Tōrei records criticism in Hakuin's lifetime that the master told tall tales. None of these witnesses was read here; the report is Waddell's, and it bears on every node below whose speaker is the hermit."@en ;
    skos:note "The work dates its own composition twice and the two do not agree with the date of the illness. The preface says the master has used the method with his students for about thirty years and is past seventy; the main text dates the visit 1710, when Hakuin was twenty-five. Waddell reconstructs the illness some years later than the work places it (p. 84). No node depends on the year."@en .

pmw:Shobogenzo a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Shōbōgenzō of Dōgen"@en, "Shōbōgenzō des Dōgen"@de ;
    skos:note "Named first by the registration and not opened in this run. Not looked for. The Yasenkanna cites Dōgen once, for a teaching he received from Rujing, to place the mind on the left palm in sitting; that saying is in Dōgen's Hōkyōki and not in the Shōbōgenzō, according to Waddell's note 116."@en .

pmw:Orategama a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Orategama of Hakuin Ekaku"@en, "Orategama des Hakuin Ekaku"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Its first page stands in the same Wikisource transcription of Shaku Dokutan's edition directly after the Yasenkanna (p. 33), where it opens with the same diagnosis, that a practitioner with a bad heart in effort falls between movement and stillness, his heart-fire rises and an illness hard to cure arises, and that true naikan is the secret of nourishing life; that line was seen and is not entered, because the work was not read. Waddell cites it in his notes as adding a fifth contemplation to the four and as having the hermit equate the five phases with the five senses. Shaw's translation, The Embossed Tea Kettle (1963), lies at archive.org under loan restriction (embossedteakettl0000haku); a further upload, in.ernet.dli.2015.139827, is without restriction and was not opened."@en .

pmw:KanzanshiSendaiKimon a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kanzanshi sendai kimon of Hakuin Ekaku"@en, "Kanzanshi sendai kimon des Hakuin Ekaku"@de ;
    skos:note "Not opened. The first printed version of the Yasenkanna story, published 1746 in Chinese as a note to a poem of Hanshan, according to Waddell's introduction. Two claims in it bear on this holding and neither was read at the wording: that nine of ten students who used the method recovered completely, and that the hermit adds a restriction on passing the method on, that it must not be divulged recklessly to others because that would harm both the one who divulges it and the hermit (Waddell, note 70). Both are what the next reader of this work should check first. No usable text layer was found: the complete works at archive.org are unreadable, see the note at pmw:Yasenkanna."@en .

pmw:Itsumadegusa a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Itsumadegusa of Hakuin Ekaku"@en, "Itsumadegusa des Hakuin Ekaku"@de ;
    skos:note "Not opened. Hakuin's late autobiography in Chinese, which carries the third version of the story. Waddell translates two passages it adds (supplementary notes A and B, pp. 118-119): an account of the hermit's death from a failed leap over a ravine, and a report of cases, monks, nuns and lay people in numbers Hakuin says he cannot count, and one named young man from Matsuzaka in Ise, who were cured of grave illness by reading the Yasenkanna and practising alone. The report of cases is the nearest thing to case material the story has; it was read only in translation and is not entered."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:JapaneseZen a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Japanese Zen"@en, "Japanischer Zen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of seated practice as enactment rather than means, together with a literature on the illnesses that practice itself produces."@en ;
    skos:note "Hakuin's account of meditation sickness is a self-warning of the strongest kind the project can hold: a text prescribing a practice and describing its casualties, with a remedy."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is in the text. The cure is argued from Chinese medicine by name, the Suwen of the Huangdi neijing and the Ming physician Li Shicai, from Zhuangzi, Mencius and Su Dongpo, and from the Tiantai manuals, the Mohe zhiguan and the Xiao zhiguan, whose story of Zhiyi curing his dying brother the text retells. The Xiao zhiguan is in this holding, traditions/tiantai.ttl, and any agreement between the two files about the mind held below the navel is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The printed text of 1757 with its preface is read in Shaku Dokutan's edition; Waddell translates Yoshizawa's edition of 2000 of the same printed text. The two earlier and later versions, in the Kanzanshi sendai kimon and in the Itsumadegusa, are separate works and declared as such, unopened."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: twenty-two printed pages, pp. 12-33, and every opening of two pages carries at least one node below."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading found that the registration could not have said. The work does not only describe the casualties of practice; it sets a term for their cure and a forfeit on missing it. The preface promises that whoever practises the contemplation will be rid of the illness in five or seven days and at most in twenty-one, or may cut off the old monk's head, and in the next sentence reports that most, not all, recovered, at a speed that depended on their diligence. The main text then has the author cured in a little less than three years. It is the only term with a forfeit this holding has met in a text prescribing a contemplation, and the same work moves the term in two directions within a few pages."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Dōgen, Shōbōgenzō; Hakuin, Yasenkanna; Hakuin, Orategama" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Yasenkanna, pmw:Shobogenzo, pmw:Orategama, pmw:KanzanshiSendaiKimon, pmw:Itsumadegusa ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 26-27" .

#
# The practice that makes the illness, and the illness
#

pmp:JapaneseZenKanriKado a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Contemplation of principle pushed beyond measure"@en, "Betrachtung des Prinzips über das Maß hinaus getrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the work names as the cause of the illness: after a first awakening, kōan practice taken up again with teeth clenched and eyes staring, food and sleep given up; in the hermit's words, contemplation of principle, kanri, beyond measure and progress that lost its proper rhythm; and, in the preface, the monks of Hakuin's assembly who went beyond measure in their inquiry and lost all restraint in their austerity."@en ;
    skos:note "The hermit's theory names the same practice twice more: contemplation that loses its measure and a will that goes too far drive the heart-fire up (p. 22), and many-pointed contemplation, takan, is wrong contemplation and is how the illness was contracted (p. 26). Leggett renders kanri as contemplation on truth, Ri-kan; Waddell as pushing yourself too hard."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 14-15, main text pp. 18-21, 22-23 and 26-27" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 89, 97, 100, 102 and 109" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), pp. 3, 6, 9 and 15" .

pmc:JapaneseZenZenbyo a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Zen sickness: the heart-fire rises and the body fails"@en, "Zenkrankheit: das Herzfeuer steigt, und der Körper versagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's own case, in the main text: before a month of the renewed effort was out, the heart-fire rose against its course and the lungs dried; both legs were as if plunged in ice and snow, both ears as if walking beside a mountain torrent; liver and gall were timid and every act was fearful; body and mind were exhausted; in sleep and waking he saw all kinds of visions; his armpits were always wet with sweat and his eyes always full of tears. He visited teachers and physicians far and wide and a hundred medicines brought no relief. The hermit names it zenbyō, Zen sickness, hard to cure by any doctor."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JapaneseZenKanriKado ;
    skos:note "A caution in the strongest form the holding knows: the text that prescribes Zen practice describes its casualty in the first person, in symptoms of which several, the cold feet, the sweat, the tears, the timidity, are visible to a bystander. It names a time, less than a month from the onset of the overstrain to the illness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 18-21" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 97 and 100" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), pp. 3 and 6" .

pmc:JapaneseZenHakkyo a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The monks who overstrain come to the brink of madness and to chronic illness of lungs and belly"@en, "Die Mönche, die sich überanstrengen, geraten an den Rand des Wahnsinns und in chronische Krankheit von Lunge und Bauch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The preface on Hakuin's assembly: those who went beyond measure in their inquiry and lost restraint in their austerity injured and dried their lungs, their water was exhausted, and they developed hernial and abdominal pains and lumps, serious and hard to cure; and over thirty years some of the monks who came, exhausted and worn out, had the heart-fire rise until they were about to go mad."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JapaneseZenKanriKado ;
    skos:note "The second half reports cases in an assembly over thirty years, not one man's illness, and its consequence, madness, is the one a third party sees first. No number of cases is given. Waddell renders the first part as some injured their lungs; the Japanese says those who went too far, without a count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 14-17" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 89 and 94" .

#
# The contemplations said to cure it
#

pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Naikan, introspective contemplation lying down before sleep"@en, "Naikan, innere Betrachtung im Liegen vor dem Schlaf"@de ;
    skos:definition "The method the preface gives to the monks. Before falling asleep and before closing the eyes, stretch both legs long and press them together hard, and let the whole primal energy of the body fill the navel, the ocean of energy, the cinnabar field, the loins, the legs and the soles of the feet; and repeat over and over the contemplation: this ocean of energy, cinnabar field, loins, legs and soles are my original face, and what nostrils has that face; my native home, and what news comes from that home; the pure land of my own mind, and what adornment has that land; the Amida of my own self, and what dharma does that Amida preach. The text calls the repetition a deliberate delusion, mōsō, to be heaped up. The hermit gives the principle of the same method: to draw the heart-fire down and fill the region from the cinnabar field to the soles of the feet."@en ;
    skos:note "Hakuin's preface counts four contemplations, and the fourth names only the ocean of energy and the cinnabar field. Waddell notes that the Orategama adds a fifth on Zhaozhou's Mu. The main text gives no lying posture and no contemplation sentences; it gives the principle and the hermit's quotations. Leggett's column does not carry the preface."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 14-15, main text pp. 26-27" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 90-92 and 109" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), pp. 14-15" .

pmc:JapaneseZenZenbyoHeiyu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Zen sickness and its attendant illnesses swept away to the root"@en, "Die Zenkrankheit und ihre Begleitleiden bis auf den Grund beseitigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work is written for: the illnesses brought on by overstrained practice, the five accumulations and six congestions, the depletion of energy and the wasting, swept away to the bottom and healed, where needles, moxa and medicine could do nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 14-17, main text pp. 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 92 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanHeiyu a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Naikan heals Zen sickness, within a stated term"@en, "Naikan heilt die Zenkrankheit, binnen einer genannten Frist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made three times. In the preface the master promises that the heaped-up contemplation will fill the loins, legs and soles with the primal energy and that in five days, seven, or at most three times seven days the illnesses will be swept away and healed, and then reports that his monks practised in secret, each saw the marvellous effect, and most recovered completely. In the main text the author says that practising the hermit's naikan on his own, in a little less than three years, without medicine, needle or moxa, all his former illnesses fell away of themselves."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenZenbyoHeiyu ;
    skos:note "The two terms in the same work differ by a factor of fifty, twenty-one days for the monks against nearly three years for the author, and the work does not remark on it. The term with its forfeit is entered at pmc:JapaneseZenRosoGaKobe, the share of the cured at pmc:JapaneseZenKoNoChisoku."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 14-17, main text pp. 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 92 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenDaigo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Great awakening six or seven times, and small ones beyond counting"@en, "Großes Erwachen sechs- oder siebenmal, und kleine ohne Zahl"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the author says came with the cure: in the same less than three years, the kōan he could not get hand or tooth into before, hard to believe, hard to pass, hard to understand and hard to enter, he penetrated to the root, with the great joy of passing through six or seven times and small awakenings beyond counting; and the preface promises the monks who practise without slackening that those with years of accumulated doubt will reach the crowning matter of Zen with a great joy, clapping their hands and laughing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 18-19, main text pp. 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 95-96 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanDaigo a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Naikan, practised with Zen, helps awakening"@en, "Naikan, zusammen mit Zen geübt, fördert das Erwachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second claim of effect: naikan not only cures but greatly helps Zen meditation, as the hermit says of the methods of the Mohe zhiguan; the master has his best students practise naikan and Zen together, working the field and fighting at once, and the more they awaken the further they press on; and the author reports his six or seven great awakenings as coming with the cure."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenDaigo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 16-19, main text pp. 26-27 and 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 94-96, 110 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), pp. 15 and 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenKokiMubyo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Past seventy without the least illness, teeth sound, sight and hearing clearer"@en, "Über siebzig ohne die geringste Krankheit, Zähne fest, Sehen und Hören klarer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's account of himself in the preface: past seventy, not a speck of illness; not a tooth loose; eyes and ears clearer year by year, so that he often forgets his spectacles; the two monthly sermons never missed; fifty or sixty assemblies of three or five hundred people, lasting fifty or seventy days, without one day's lecture cancelled; body and mind sound and his strength far above what it was at twenty or thirty."@en ;
    skos:note "A claim about a named, public person, at a stated age, in particulars a contemporary could check: the teeth, the spectacles, the record of the assemblies. The main text adds the same claim in the same words, past seventy without a speck of illness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 16-17, main text pp. 32-33" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 94-95 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanKokiMubyo a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Naikan kept the author free of illness into old age"@en, "Naikan hielt den Verfasser bis ins Alter frei von Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author ascribes his health past seventy entirely to the marvellous effect of naikan, in the preface, and in the main text to the lingering benefit of the divine method."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenKokiMubyo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 16-17, main text pp. 32-33" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 95 and 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenBetsuSezu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "No socks and no brazier in the depth of winter, where the soles were ice before"@en, "Keine Strümpfe und kein Kohlebecken im tiefsten Winter, wo die Fußsohlen früher Eis waren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closing claim of the main text: where once, wearing two or three pairs of socks, the soles of his feet were always as if soaked in ice and snow, now in the coldest days of the three winter months he wears no socks and uses no brazier, and attributes it to the lingering benefit of the method."@en ;
    skos:note "The one claim of the work whose before and after are the same measure, cold in the soles of the feet, and the cold feet were one of the symptoms of the illness at pmc:JapaneseZenZenbyo. Waddell renders the season as the third month; the Japanese has the three months of winter, as Leggett does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 30-33" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanBetsuSezu a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Drawing the energy down into the soles warms them for good"@en, "Die Energie in die Fußsohlen hinabzuziehen wärmt sie auf Dauer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author ascribes the warmth of his feet in winter to the method he practised, the drawing of the heart-fire down to the cinnabar field and the soles of the feet."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenBetsuSezu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 30-33" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 116" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 22" .

pmc:JapaneseZenSejuSanbyakusai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "A lifespan of three hundred years for those of middle and lower capacity, the rest beyond reckoning"@en, "Eine Lebensspanne von dreihundert Jahren für die mittlerer und geringer Anlage, die übrige unberechenbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the master says the sage who taught him told him: this is the divine art of the immortals for long life without death; those of middle and lower capacity will live three hundred years, and for the rest it cannot be reckoned. The hermit in the main text promises more modestly that diligent practice will bring a marvellous effect and that long life too may be hoped for."@en ;
    skos:note "The work refuses its own promise in the same breath: three years later the master reflects that even eight hundred years like Peng Zu would make him only a corpse-guarding ghost, entered at pmc:JapaneseZenShushiki. Leggett renders the hermit's line as it may well be that you will never close your eyes in death; the Japanese, kyūshi mo mata kisshitsubeshi, says long life may be expected, not deathlessness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 16-17, main text pp. 20-23" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 93 and 101" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 7" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanSeju a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Naikan, the art of the immortals, lengthens life to three hundred years"@en, "Naikan, die Kunst der Unsterblichen, verlängert das Leben auf dreihundert Jahre"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the sage reported in the preface, that the secret of naikan is the divine art of long life and gives three hundred years to those of middle and lower capacity."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNaikan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenSejuSanbyakusai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), preface pp. 16-17" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 93" .

pmp:JapaneseZenTandenShuichi a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Storing the vast energy in the cinnabar field over months and years"@en, "Die gewaltige Energie über Monate und Jahre im Zinnoberfeld bewahren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hermit's account of refining the true elixir: when the six desires are removed and the five senses each forget their office, the undivided primal energy fills the space before one's eyes; this, Mencius's vast energy, is to be led down and stored in the navel, the ocean of energy and the cinnabar field, kept over the years, guarded until it is one and nourished until it goes nowhere else."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 24-25" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 105-106" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 11" .

pmc:JapaneseZenDaikantan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The great returned elixir: oneself the immortal not born before heaven and earth"@en, "Das große zurückgekehrte Elixier: man selbst als der Unsterbliche, nicht vor Himmel und Erde geboren"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the storing is said to end in: one morning the elixir furnace is overturned, and within, without and between, in all eight directions, everything is a single great returned elixir; then for the first time one realises that one is oneself the true great immortal of long life, not born before heaven and earth and not dying after empty space."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 24-25" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 106-107" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 11" .

pmc:JapaneseZenShuichiDaikantan a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Storing the energy for years ends in the great returned elixir"@en, "Das jahrelange Bewahren der Energie endet im großen zurückgekehrten Elixier"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hermit's claim that the energy stored and guarded in the cinnabar field over months and years issues one morning in the overturning of the furnace and the realisation of oneself as the immortal."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenTandenShuichi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenDaikantan ;
    skos:note "A claim with a stated duration, months and years, and no term. The hermit introduces it with the story of Shitai, who would pass the secret only to a vessel of the highest kind, and of the Yellow Emperor, who received it after twenty-one days of purification: a restriction on passing on, told as precedent and not laid on the hearer, who is being told it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 24-25" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 105-107" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 11" .

pmp:JapaneseZenNansoNoHo a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The soft-butter method, nanso no hō"@en, "Die Methode der weichen Butter, nanso no hō"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hermit's contemplation for one who in concentration finds the four elements out of balance and body and mind exhausted. Imagine a lump of soft butter, pure in colour and scent, the size of a duck's egg, set on the crown of the head; its taste and savour are subtle, it moistens the whole skull and seeps down, soaking in turn both shoulders and arms, the breasts and chest, lungs, liver, bowels and stomach, spine and buttocks; the accumulations and pains in the chest go down with the mind like water running downhill, with an audible sound, circulate through the body, warm both legs and stop at the soles of the feet. Then the second contemplation: the overflow pools and warms as in a bath that a good physician has filled with a decoction of fragrant herbs, in which one sits from the navel down."@en ;
    skos:note "The text sets the contemplation in concentration, jōchū, for a practitioner already sitting, and not before sleep as the preface sets the naikan. Waddell (note 124) reports that Itō Kazuo found similar methods in Buddhist sutras and that a butter method stands in the Zhi chanbing biyao fa, Taishō vol. 15 no. 620; that was not read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 113-114" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 19" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNansoBanno a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "No illness not cured, no virtue not gained, no sagehood not reached"@en, "Keine Krankheit ungeheilt, keine Tugend unerworben, keine Heiligkeit unerreicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the butter method is said to give: body and mind in better order than at twenty or thirty, the accumulations melted, bowels and stomach in harmony, the skin glowing unawares; and if one practises without slackening, what illness will not be cured, what virtue not gathered, what immortal state not reached, what way not accomplished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 114" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 19" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNansoBannoYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The butter method cures every illness and accomplishes every way"@en, "Die Buttermethode heilt jede Krankheit und vollendet jeden Weg"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hermit's claim for the soft-butter method, with its speed laid on the practitioner: the swiftness or slowness of the effect depends only on the fineness or coarseness of his practice."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNansoNoHo ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenNansoBanno ;
    skos:note "A claim universal in its object, every illness, every virtue, every way, and therefore with no illness it excludes. The only thing that could fail is the speed, and the speed is laid on the practitioner in the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 114" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 19" .

pmc:JapaneseZenTodaiNashi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "No freezing in thin clothes in midwinter, no hunger after months without grain"@en, "Kein Erfrieren in dünner Kleidung im tiefsten Winter, kein Hunger nach Monaten ohne Getreide"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the hermit says of himself: a sickly youth given up by the doctors, he received the butter method, and before a month was out most of his illnesses were gone; he lost count of months and years and of his own age; now, with two or three thin cotton robes, the winter cold does not freeze his bowels, and though mountain food fails and he takes no grain for months at a time, he never feels cold or hunger."@en ;
    skos:note "The speaker is the hermit, whose meeting with the author Waddell's introduction reports as most probably not having happened (see pmw:Yasenkanna). Before a month was out most were gone: the Japanese has taihan, the greater part, as Leggett renders it; Waddell has almost totally."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 114-115" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 20" .

pmc:JapaneseZenNansoTodaiNashi a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The butter contemplation keeps the hermit from cold and hunger"@en, "Die Butterbetrachtung bewahrt den Einsiedler vor Kälte und Hunger"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hermit ascribes his freedom from cold and hunger to the power of this contemplation: is it not all the power of this contemplation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenNansoNoHo ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenTodaiNashi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 30-31" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 115" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 20" .

#
# The practices the hermit quotes from Chinese authors
#

pmp:JapaneseZenWashinDoki a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Harmonising the spirit and guiding the energy, lying with a feather on the nose"@en, "Den Geist harmonisieren und die Energie leiten, liegend mit einer Feder auf der Nase"@de ;
    skos:definition "The method the hermit quotes as Peng Zu's: shut oneself deep in a closed room, set the bed, warm the mat, a pillow two and a half sun high, lie straight on the back, close the eyes, shut the heart-energy within the breast, put a goose feather on the nose, and breathe so that it does not move for three hundred breaths."@en ;
    skos:note "Quoted and not taught in the author's own voice. Waddell (note 121) reports that Yoshizawa found a similar passage in Su Dongpo ascribed to Sun Simiao."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 112" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 17" .

pmc:JapaneseZenKanshoFushin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Cold and heat cannot invade, bees and scorpions cannot poison, a life of three hundred and sixty years"@en, "Kälte und Hitze dringen nicht ein, Bienen und Skorpione vergiften nicht, ein Leben von dreihundertsechzig Jahren"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the quoted method is said to give: when the ears hear nothing and the eyes see nothing, cold and heat cannot invade, bees and scorpions cannot poison, the lifespan is three hundred and sixty years, and one is near to being a true man."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 112" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 17" .

pmc:JapaneseZenWashinDokiYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Three hundred motionless breaths make one proof against cold, heat and poison"@en, "Dreihundert reglose Atemzüge machen gefeit gegen Kälte, Hitze und Gift"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the hermit quotes as Peng Zu's, that the breath held so still that a feather does not stir, for three hundred breaths, brings the state in which cold, heat and sting do not harm and life reaches three hundred and sixty years."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenWashinDoki ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenKanshoFushin ;
    skos:note "The only claim of the work with an outward test built into the practice: a feather that does not move. The claimed outcome, proof against a sting, is also the only one a third party could produce on demand. The work does not say anyone did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 112" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 17" .

pmp:JapaneseZenSusoku a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Counting the breaths on an empty stomach, from one to a thousand"@en, "Die Atemzüge mit leerem Magen zählen, von eins bis tausend"@de ;
    skos:definition "The method the hermit quotes as Su Dongpo's: eat only when hungry and stop before full; walk at leisure until the belly is empty; then enter a quiet room, sit upright in silence and count the out- and in-breaths, from one to ten, ten to a hundred, a hundred to a thousand, until the body is still and the mind as quiet as empty space; and continue long."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, p. 112" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 18" .

pmc:JapaneseZenIssokuJishi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "The breath stops of itself, rises like mist from the pores, and the illnesses of beginningless time fall away"@en, "Der Atem hört von selbst auf, steigt wie Nebel aus den Poren, und die Krankheiten anfangsloser Zeit fallen ab"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the counting is said to bring: after long practice a breath stops of itself and neither goes out nor comes in; then it rises like cloud and mist from the eighty-four thousand pores, and one clearly realises that the illnesses of beginningless time have gone of themselves and every obstruction is removed, like a blind man whose eyes suddenly open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 112-113" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 18" .

pmc:JapaneseZenSusokuYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Yasenkanna ;
    rdfs:label "Long counting of the breaths ends in the breath that stops and the illnesses that go"@en, "Langes Zählen der Atemzüge endet im stillstehenden Atem und im Weichen der Krankheiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the hermit quotes as Su Dongpo's, that breath-counting continued long brings the breath to a stop of itself and with it the end of all illness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JapaneseZenSusoku ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JapaneseZenIssokuJishi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JapaneseZen ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin Ekaku, Yasenkanna, in Shaku Dokutan, ed., Hakuin zenji hōgoroku (Tokyo: Ōshima Seishindō), pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Hakuin's Yasenkanna, trans. Norman Waddell, The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1, pp. 112-113" ;
    dcterms:source "Yasenkanna, trans. Trevor Leggett, in F. Lecut, Master Hakuin's Yasen Kanna, 2 translations (compilation, 2011), p. 18" .

# ANCHOR-JZ-2
